            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Arrian's Philosophy</strong><br>\nAside from his historical works, Arrian was deeply interested in philosophy. He was a devoted student of Epictetus, a famous Stoic philosopher of his time. Arrian's \"Discourses of Epictetus,\" compiled from his notes, is one of the principal sources of modern understanding of Stoicism, emphasizing virtue, wisdom, and endurance.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Political Career</strong><br>\nArrian's acumen and experiences earned him political favor, leading to a successful career in the Roman administrative system. He served as the governor of Cappadocia, a large province in Asia Minor, where he managed to secure the region's frontier against invasions. His tenure as governor was marked by both military and civil accomplishments.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Arrian's Impact on History</strong><br>\nThrough his works, Arrian has significantly contributed to the historical understanding of Alexander the Great's conquests. His accounts are considered some of the most reliable sources on Alexander's expeditions, surpassing those of many other historians due to the extensive use of primary sources and his systematic approach to writing.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
