          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>His Works as Educational Texts</strong><br>\nEducators and scholars value Arrian's writings for their educational merit, using them to teach about ancient history and philosophy. His accounts of Alexander's reign are commonly used as primary texts for understanding the Hellenistic period. They provide valuable insights into the political and military strategies of ancient times.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Influence</strong><br>\nArrian's works remained influential well beyond his death. They provide a connection to the classical age of Greece, serving as a bridge between the Hellenistic and Roman periods. His influence extended to both the literary and academic fields, helping to shape the way history was recorded and understood in later centuries.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Modern Reception and Relevance</strong><br>\nToday, historians and readers continue to study Arrian's texts, which offer a unique perspective on antiquity. His commitment to factual reporting and his analytical approach make his writings a staple in classical studies. The continued relevance of his work is a testament to his skill as a historian and his understanding of the cultural dynamics of his time.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
